+## R20 / alpha12 - Survival-scale resource efficiency
+
+Goal: ordinary survival servers with thousands of players should be able to keep 20 TPS when hardware, plugins and world load are adequate, without lowering gameplay correctness.
+
+Implementation direction:
+
+1. Build a tick-local interest graph for players and hotspots. Shared read-only fanout preparation is allowed; hiding entities, skipping entity ticks, dropping packets or changing chunk order is not allowed.
+2. Add CPU/memory-aware resource budgeting. Ordered commits and plugin-visible mutations always keep priority; invisible prepare work spends only remaining safe headroom.
+3. Add global fairness across players/worlds/regions/arenas. A single spawn, farm, raid or event hotspot must not starve the rest of the server.
+4. Add tick-local immutable read-only cache. Duplicate visibility/chunk/fanout computations can be shared in one tick, but live world state is never cached beyond the tick boundary.
+5. Expose `/agc survival-scale` and `/agc resources` so operators can see whether the server is CPU-bound, heap-bound, hotspot-bound or fairness-bound.
+
+Validation gate before beta:
+
+- Plugin event order audit with ProtocolLib, ViaVersion, Geyser, Multiverse, Citizens, CoreProtect and WorldEdit/FAWE.
+- Survival stress profile: spawn hotspot, moving players, chunk exploration, redstone farms, villagers, item entities, portals and cross-world travel.
+- Resource profile: CPU saturation, heap pressure, Netty flush backlog, chunk IO pressure and plugin scheduler backlog.
+- SLO: 20 TPS target, no interactive packet deadline violations, no chunk FIFO head skip, no entity tick skip, no off-thread Bukkit mutation.
